---

Here is everything you need to know about the `registerSidebarTab` API, drawn directly from live source files on your machine.

---

## 1. The `app` Import (Exact Statement)

All non-bundled custom node JS files use the same import. The relative depth varies by where the file lives inside the node's directory, but the target path is always `../../../scripts/app.js` from the ComfyUI root's perspective:

```js
// From a web/js/*.js file one level deep:
import { app } from "../../scripts/app.js";

// From a web/comfyui/*.js file one level deep:
import { app } from "../../scripts/app.js";

// From a deeper file (e.g. web/js/easy/easy.js, three levels deep):
import { app } from "../../../../scripts/app.js";

// The canonical form — from testRunner.js, which is three levels deep:
import { app } from "../../../scripts/app.js";
```

The source of truth is `G:/COMFYUI_Database/Custom_Nodes/comfyui-videohelpersuite/testframework/web/js/testRunner.js` line 2:
```js
import {app} from "../../../scripts/app.js";
```

For `api.js` (same pattern, same path depth):
```js
import {api} from "../../../scripts/api.js";
```

The path `scripts/app.js` is the ComfyUI root-relative path — the actual file lives at `<ComfyUI_root>/web/scripts/app.js` (not present locally since ComfyUI itself isn't installed under `G:/COMFYUI_Database/` directly, it's served from another location). The browser resolves it relative to the origin.

---

## 2. `registerSidebarTab` — Full Call Signature

### Minimal working example (plain JS, no framework)

From `G:/COMFYUI_Database/Custom_Nodes/comfyui-videohelpersuite/testframework/web/js/testRunner.js` (lines 58-96):

```js
import {app} from "../../../scripts/app.js";
import {api} from "../../../scripts/api.js";

let testSidebar = {
    id: 'VHStest',           // string — unique ID for this tab
    title: 'VHS Test',       // string — displayed in sidebar tooltip/label
    icon: 'VHSTestIcon',     // string — CSS class applied to the icon element
    type: 'custom',          // string — must be 'custom' for custom HTML tabs
    render: (el) => {        // function — receives an HTMLElement container
        el.innerHTML = `Select a folder containing tests
        <input>
        Or select a single test
        <input>
        `;
        // Work with el directly — it is a live HTMLElement
        const folderInput = el.children[0];
        const fileInput = el.children[1];
        // ... attach event listeners etc.
    }
};

app.extensionManager.registerSidebarTab(testSidebar);
```

### With Vue 3 + createApp (comfyui-easy-use pattern)

From `G:/COMFYUI_Database/Custom_Nodes/comfyui-easy-use/web_version/v2/easyuse.js` (the App component's `onMounted`):

```js
// These are minified aliases — in source they are:
// s = app (the ComfyUI app object, accessed via window.comfyAPI.app.app in the bundle)
// c = SIDEBAR_ID constant (a string like "easyuse.nodesmap")
// u = i18n translation function
// U = createApp (Vue's createApp)
// L = defineComponent
// De = the Vue component to mount

s.extensionManager.registerSidebarTab({
    id: c,                      // string — unique tab ID
    icon: "pi pi-sitemap",      // string — PrimeIcons class OR custom CSS class
    title: u("NodesMap", true), // string — tab title (shown in header)
    tooltip: u("NodesMap", true),// string — hover tooltip
    type: "custom",             // string — always 'custom' for custom tabs
    render: (el) => {           // function — el is the HTMLElement sidebar panel
        el.style.height = "100%";
        // Mount a Vue component into el:
        U(L(De, {}), el);       // createApp(defineComponent(NodesMap), el)
    }
});
```

In readable form, what the render function does:
```js
render: (el) => {
    el.style.height = "100%";
    // createApp is from Vue 3
    createApp(MyVueComponent, {}).mount(el);
}
```

---

## 3. Full Parameter Reference for `registerSidebarTab`

| Field | Type | Required | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `id` | `string` | Yes | Unique identifier. Use a namespaced string like `"myplugin.sidebar"` |
| `title` | `string` | Yes | Displayed as the panel heading/tooltip |
| `icon` | `string` | Yes | CSS class applied to the icon. Use `"pi pi-..."` for PrimeIcons (ComfyUI uses PrimeVue). For custom icons: define a CSS class that sets `content` via `:before` (see VHS example below) |
| `tooltip` | `string` | No | Hover tooltip text. Falls back to `title` if omitted |
| `type` | `string` | Yes | Must be `"custom"` for a custom render panel |
| `render` | `function(el: HTMLElement): void` | Yes | Called once with the panel `HTMLElement`. You own this element. Set content, style it, mount frameworks into it. |

### Custom icon CSS pattern (from VHS testRunner):
```js
let iconOverride = document.createElement("style");
iconOverride.innerHTML = `.VHSTestIcon:before {content: '🧪';}`;
document.body.append(iconOverride);
// then use icon: 'VHSTestIcon' in the tab config
```

---

## 4. The `render` Function — What `el` Is

`el` is a plain `HTMLElement` — the sidebar panel container div. It is:
- Already mounted in the DOM when `render` is called
- Sized to fill the sidebar panel height (you may need to set `el.style.height = "100%"` yourself)
- Yours to mutate directly — set `innerHTML`, append children, mount a Vue/React app into it

Three confirmed patterns:

**Pattern A — raw innerHTML + DOM:**
```js
render: (el) => {
    el.innerHTML = `<div>My sidebar content</div>`;
    el.querySelector("div").addEventListener("click", () => { /* ... */ });
}
```

**Pattern B — Vue 3 createApp:**
```js
render: (el) => {
    el.style.height = "100%";
    createApp(MyVueComponent).mount(el);
}
```

**Pattern C — call inside registerExtension setup (deferred):**
```js
app.registerExtension({
    name: "MyExtension",
    async setup() {
        app.extensionManager.registerSidebarTab({
            id: "my.tab",
            icon: "pi pi-star",
            title: "My Tab",
            type: "custom",
            render: (el) => {
                el.textContent = "Hello from sidebar";
            }
        });
    }
});
```

---

## 5. Where `extensionManager` Lives on `app`

From the extensions bundle (`extensions-ByfbHFiH.js`, minified access pattern):
```js
// The bundle accesses app via window.comfyAPI:
let app = window.comfyAPI?.app?.app || null;
// Then:
app.extensionManager.registerSidebarTab({...});
```

In plain extension JS files (non-bundled), `app` imported from `scripts/app.js` exposes `extensionManager` directly:
```js
import { app } from "../../scripts/app.js";
app.extensionManager.registerSidebarTab({...});
// Also available on extensionManager:
// app.extensionManager.toast.add(...)  — toast notifications
// app.extensionManager.setting.get(id) — settings
// app.extensionManager.workflow        — workflow service
```

---

## 6. Complete Minimal Working Pattern (copy-paste ready)

```js
import { app } from "../../scripts/app.js";

// Optional: custom icon via CSS
const style = document.createElement("style");
style.textContent = `.my-sidebar-icon:before { content: '🔧'; }`;
document.head.appendChild(style);

app.extensionManager.registerSidebarTab({
    id: "myplugin.panel",
    icon: "my-sidebar-icon",   // OR use "pi pi-cog" for a built-in PrimeIcon
    title: "My Panel",
    tooltip: "Open My Panel",
    type: "custom",
    render(el) {
        el.style.height = "100%";
        el.style.padding = "8px";
        el.innerHTML = `<h3>My Sidebar</h3><p>Content goes here.</p>`;
    }
});
```

